bb{R}^3$ has first coordinate $2$ and third coordinate $-1$?","options":["$(2,-1)$","$(0,2,-1)$","$(-1,0,2)$","$(2,0,-1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7f0e6fcd78dc9d8c6bf32f7e","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch13_coordinate_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $(a,3)=(2,b)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, what are $a$ and $b$?","options":["$a=3$ and $b=2$","$a=b=2$","$a=b=3$","$a=2$ and $b=3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4b194ccb4a8634b8c9b29206","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch13_coordinate_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is $(1,2)$ not the same coordinate vector as $(2,1)$?","options":["The first and second coordinates have been interchanged.","They have different lengths, so they cannot be equal.","They point in opposite directions along the same line.","Their coordinate sums differ, so the two vectors differ."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b1610e424efc7c09de8f4872","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch13_coordinate_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a coordinate description allow us to do with a vector equation?","options":["Reduce it to one equation comparing the vectors’ lengths","Break it into scalar equations for individual entries","Reduce it to one equation comparing coordinate sums","Check it in the first coordinate and infer the rest"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"96fba3fd1eff2eef8af3207b","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_bat